Specifying tight bound is already how TLS 1.3 is defined:

CipherSuite cipher_suites<2..2^16-2>;
SignatureScheme supported_signature_algorithms<2..2^16-2>;
struct {
...
Extension extensions<0..2^16-2>;
} NewSessionTicket;


On Friday, 9 May 2025 19:22:03 CEST, David Benjamin wrote:
> FWIW, I don't think this is an error in the specification per=20
> se. Both 2^16-2 and 2^16-1 to describe the exact same=20
> structure, precisely because a length of 2^16-1 is impossible.=20
> One isn't inherently more correct than the other. The question=20
> is which is clearer, the loose bound or the tight bound.
>
> Indeed I would argue that the loose bound, 2^16-1, is a=20
> _better_ way to describe the structure than the tight bound,=20
> 2^16-2. (I would also argue that a minimum length of 1 is a=20
> better way to describe it than 2, but that's less crucial.) The=20
> bounds here communicate two things to the TLS implementor:
>
> 1. What size of length prefix to use, which is=20
> implicitly determined by the upper bound
> 2. What checks to apply on the length you've parsed out
>
> The key thing to keep in mind is that the size of the length=20
> prefix already bounds the lengths (here 0..2^16-1), so any=20
> checks you write from (2) are in addition to the bounds you get=20
> from the length prefix. When the written bounds are equal to the=20
> implicit bounds, you don't need to write a check. Where either=20
> upper or lower bound are different (e.g. opaque=20
> legacy_session_id<0..32>), you need to write an extra check.
>
> That is, you would parse opaque legacy_session_id<0..32> like this:
>
>   legacy_session_id =3D client_hello.read_u8_length_prefix()
>   if len(legacy_session_id) > 32:
>     raise DecodeError()
>
> But you would parse opaque extension_data<0..2^16-1> like this:
>
>   extension_data =3D extension.read_u16_length_prefix()
>   # No need to check additional bounds because u16 is already <0..2^16-1>
>
> Now, the NamedGroup field is a u16-prefixed list of at least=20
> one NamedGroup. We actually just want to parse it like this:
>
>   named_group_list =3D extension_data.read_u16_length_prefix()
>   if len(named_group_list) =3D=3D 0:
>     raise DecodeError()
>
> But when we write it as NamedGroup named_group_list<2..2^16-2>,=20
> it *looks like* you have to parse it like:
>
>   named_group_list =3D extension_data.read_u16_length_prefix()
>   if len(named_group_list) < 2 or len(named_group_list) > 2**16 - 2:
>     raise DecodeError()
>
> To know that these do the same thing you have to observe that:
> 1. It is not possible to have a NamedGroup of length less than 2
> 2. NamedGroup is always exactly two bytes, so it is not=20
> possible to have a NamedGroup list of length 2^16-1.
>
> This is extra work for the implementer and makes it more likely=20
> that they'll misunderstand it. Or perhaps they'll get used to=20
> the TLSWG writing things in this weird way and miss a case when=20
> they *do* need to enforce an upper bound. This is particularly=20
> fraught because TLS structures are variable-length. Here is a=20
> contrived example to demonstrate the silliness of this=20
> convention:
>
> enum { len1000(1},  len30001(2) } Type;
> struct TwoPossibleLengths {
>     Type type;
>     select (type) {
>        case len1000: opaque padding[999];
>        case len30001: opaque padding[30000];
>     }=20
> };
>
> struct {
>   TwoPossibleLengths list<1000..65002>;
> } Confusing;
>
> If you look at this field, you might think that this is=20
> actually imposing some limit that is tighter than its length=20
> prefix, and that you need to check more things. In reality, it's=20
> not. It's fairly clear that the minimum length of=20
> a TwoPossibleLengths is 1000. It's much less obvious that the=20
> maximum length of a series of TwoPossibleLengths, constrained to=20
> be under 2^16-1, is 65002. So I think this is a far, far better=20
> way to write this same structure.
>
> struct {
>   TwoPossibleLengths list<1..2^16-1>;
> } MuchMuchClearer;
>
> It describes the exact same structure, but now it's obvious the=20
> intended parse was that you check the length is non-zero and=20
> leave the upper-bound set based on its length prefix. Now,=20
> obviously this is a contrived example and that instance of=20
> knapsack is quite a bit harder than the NamedGroup instance. But=20
> I think it demonstrates why the simpler loose bound convention=20
> is better for readers of the specification than the one that=20
> relies on the reader to do more math.
>
> David
>
>
